{"name":"Moire","hex":"#beadb0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moire","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Originally, a fine textile fabric made of the hair of an Asiatic goat.","Any textile fabric to which a watered appearance is given."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moire"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moire (fabric)","blocks":[{"text":"Moire, less often moiré, is a textile with a wavy (watered) appearance produced mainly from silk, but also wool, cotton, and rayon. The watered appearance is usually created by the finishing technique called calendering. Moiré effects are also achieved by certain weaves, such as varying the tension in the warp and weft of the weave. Silk treated in this way is sometimes called watered silk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Moireband.jpg/330px-Moireband.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moire_(fabric)","wikidata":"Q1752512"},{"title":"Moiré pattern","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, physics, and art, a moiré pattern or moiré fringe is a large-scale wave interference pattern that can be produced when a partially opaque ruled pattern with transparent gaps is overlaid on another similar pattern. For the moiré interference pattern to appear, the two patterns must not be completely identical, but rather displaced, rotated, or have slightly different pitch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Moir%C3%A9_pattern.svg/330px-Moir%C3%A9_pattern.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moir%C3%A9_pattern","wikidata":"Q26468"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Watered_silk.jpg/330px-Watered_silk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Watered_silk.jpg/330px-Watered_silk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gown of moire or watered silk, 1840–44","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWatered_silk.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Peter_der-Grosse_1838.jpg/500px-Peter_der-Grosse_1838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Peter_der-Grosse_1838.jpg/500px-Peter_der-Grosse_1838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peter the Great wearing the insignia of the Order of St. Andrew and a moire ribbon sash","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeter_der-Grosse_1838.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Mahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg/500px-Mahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Mahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg/1280px-Mahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Mahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg/500px-Mahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roger Cardinal Mahony (left) wearing a ferraiolo of watered silk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMahony%2C_Alberts%2C_Gomez.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Divers_-_Illustrated_London_News_Feb_6_1873-2.PNG/500px-Divers_-_Illustrated_London_News_Feb_6_1873-2.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Divers_-_Illustrated_London_News_Feb_6_1873-2.PNG/1280px-Divers_-_Illustrated_London_News_Feb_6_1873-2.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par